            <h1>Topographic Maps as Risk Interfaces: Visualising Geomorphological Hazards for Outdoor Decision-Making</h1>

            <div class="survey-description"><p dir="ltr"><em>Welcome. Thank you for agreeing to take part in this survey.</em></p>

<p dir="ltr"><em>My name is Federica Ballardini and this survey is part of my Master’s thesis in Cartography. I am investigating the effect of different visualisations of rockfall on digital topographic map-based decision-making.</em></p>
</div>

            <div class="survey-welcome"><p dir="ltr"><b id="docs-internal-guid-0400720f-7fff-7af7-0420-297d741f5576">Why is this important? </b>Mountain environments are increasingly affected by environmental change and natural hazards such as rockfall and slope instability.&nbsp;A study by the Austrian Road Safety Board (KFV) shows that 760 km of Austria’s hiking infrastructure is at high risk of rockfall and an average of 61 injuries and 3 fatalities every year were caused by rockfall between 2019 and 2023 (Fian et al., 2024). Communicating this information effectively on maps can support safer and more informed route decisions.</p>

<p dir="ltr">The purpose of this survey is to understand how people interpret and interact with different map designs, including maps with hazard-related information.</p>

<p dir="ltr">Participation is <strong>voluntary and anonymous</strong>. The survey will take approximately <strong>15-20 minutes</strong> to complete.</p>

<p dir="ltr"><b>NOTE: </b>The survey is best viewed on a <b>laptop. </b>If you are using your<b> smartphone, <u>make sure to zoom out so you can see the maps fully.</u></b></p>

<p dir="ltr"><b id="docs-internal-guid-0400720f-7fff-7af7-0420-297d741f5576">Thank you for your time and contribution to this research.</b></p>

<p dir="ltr">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

